module bindings.python.urlSchemeDenylistWithoutDecoding;

// urllib.parse.urlparse and urlsplit answer with the components of the
// URL as it stands: percent-escapes and HTML entities stay inside the
// components, and the consumer of the URL -- a browser following a
// redirect or rendering a link -- decodes them after the check. A
// validator that decides whether a URL belongs to the site by parsing
// it and comparing host and path, while the only scheme filtering it
// does is a comparison against script-scheme literals ('javascript:'
// and its browser-scripting siblings), carries both halves of the
// weakness a denylist names: every scheme that is not enumerated is
// allowed, and every payload that decodes to something else after the
// check survives it -- '%2e%2e' walks a path-prefix comparison out of
// the site as surely as a scheme the list never named.
//
// The gate is the conjunction of the three readings, in one function.
// A URL parse call alone is ordinary URL handling, and a script-scheme
// literal alone may be defending something other than a redirect
// target; together, in the function that also decides containment,
// they are the membership check a redirect target passes through. The
// literal is read from the function's own expression tokens -- the
// comparison that tests it -- so a denylist compared in a sibling
// function credits nothing here, and a module-level denylist table the
// function iterates does not satisfy the clause either: the iteration
// compares a variable, and the literal is not in the function at all.
//
// The decode step is credited by operation rather than by name.
// Obtaining the decoded form (urllib.parse.unquote and unquote_plus,
// html.unescape) and re-encoding the value on the way out
// (urllib.parse.quote) both make the comparison run on the form the
// consumer will see, so a validator that does either is not reported.
// A validator that allowlists schemes and never decodes still is: the
// scheme half of the weakness is closed and the escape half is not,
// and the path-prefix comparison remains walkable with encoded
// segments.
//
// Residual, in the silent direction: a decode or quote call on an
// unrelated value in the same function silences the report, and a
// validator that normalizes by other means -- replacing the escapes by
// hand, recursing on a rewritten copy built without the standard
// decoders -- is not credited. The operation spellings credited are
// the standard library's, which is the reliable subset.
binding pyUrlSchemeDenylistWithoutDecoding {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["urlparse", "urlsplit"])
    and containsAny(node.context.expr, ["javascript:", "vbscript:", "jscript:", "livescript:"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["unescape", "quote"])
  emit issue code.WeakRedirectUrlValidation at node
}
